Cell["\<\
Start reading from this Mathematica tutorial in Wolfram Language \
Documentation Center: CUDALink/tutorial/Programming\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9754255158483467`*^9, 3.9754255450922337`*^9}, {
  3.9754256004128056`*^9, 
  3.975425662091942*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9d488be2-081d-a84a-9eb3-\
8cb03549529e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "CUDA\[CloseCurlyQuote]s programming is based on the data parallel model. \
From a high-level standpoint, the problem is first partitioned onto hundreds \
or thousands of threads for computation. If the following is your \
computation:\n\nOutputData = Table[fun[InputData[[i, j]]], {i, 10000}, {j, \
10000}]\nthen in the CUDA programming paradigm, this computation is \
equivalent to:\nCUDALaunch[fun, InputData, OutputData, {10000, 10000}]\n\n\
where ",
 Cell[BoxData["fun"], "InlineCode",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "89bbce68-81d5-9440-bcd5-dc16796bfab1"],
 " is a computation function. The above launches 10000\[Times]10000 threads, \
passes their indices to each thread, applying the function to ",
 Cell[BoxData["InputData"], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "ba54808c-22f2-5c45-b0d0-65a1f281f960"],
 ", and places the results in ",
 Cell[BoxData["OutputData"], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "4c013efe-95c1-9147-8db9-4506daebf81b"],
 ". ",
 StyleBox["CUDALink",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "\[CloseCurlyQuote]s equivalence to ",
 Cell[BoxData["CUDALaunch"], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "7d81497b-c236-d649-adfc-d3e956d8ea29"],
 " is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["CUDAFunction"]], "paclet:CUDALink/ref/CUDAFunction", 
    "CUDALink Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "5eb88fc7-8c5f-6341-9960-173ba67b30f4"],
 ".\n\nGlobal Memory\nThe most abundant (but slowest) memory available on the \
GPU. This is the memory advertised on the packaging\[LongDash]128, 256, or \
512 MB. All threads can access elements in global memory, although for \
performance reasons these accesses tend to be kept to a minimum and have \
further constrictions on them.\nThe performance constrictions on global \
memory have been relaxed on recent hardware and will likely be relaxed even \
further. The general rule is that performance is deteriorated if global \
memory is accessed more than once.\nTexture Memory\nTexture memory resides in \
the same location as global memory, but it is read-only. Texture memory does \
not suffer for the performance deterioration found in global memory. On the \
flip side, only ",
 StyleBox["char", "InlineCode",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["int", "InlineCode",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", and ",
 StyleBox["float", "InlineCode",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " are supported types.\nConstant Memory\nFast constant memory that is \
accessible from any thread in the grid. The memory is cached, but limited to \
64 KB globally.\nShared Memory\nFast memory that is local to a specific \
block. On current hardware, the amount of shared memory is limited to 16 KB \
per block.\n"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975425814241146*^9, 3.975425855480097*^9}, {
  3.9754258982131176`*^9, 3.975425900119982*^9}, {3.975426484006817*^9, 
