Cell["\<\
We can now expect that the code really runs on the GPU as expected. I could \
see from Task Manager a significant spike in GPU load while running the code. \
The result from the last part of the code was like this: 
Testing Extension Logic on single word...
Processing 1 words. Extensions: Left=6, Right=6
Found 0 non-extendable words.
Result: The word WAS successfully extended (Solver found a solution).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9759143617014313`*^9, 
  3.975914389604759*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6d8f2f24-cd12-f844-ab59-\
908ed104475b"],

Cell["\<\
That is excellent news! Seeing that GPU load spike confirms that the driver \
is finally accepting the native sm_120 binary and executing the kernel on \
your Blackwell hardware.

The output matches our expectations perfectly:

Found 0 non-extendable words: This means the test word \
aaabaaacccbccaabbbaccbcc was successfully extended (a solution was found).

Logic Verification: Since aa2fFindNonExtendableWords returns the original \
words that fail, an empty list here is the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]success\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] state for an extendable word.

Now that the pipeline is stable and verified:

Ready for Scale: You can now load your full list of ~500,000 words into \
Mathematica.

Execution: Simply call aa2fFindNonExtendableWords[yourBigList, 6, 6].

Performance: With 500k words, the GPU will process them in batches (the code \
handles stride padding automatically). If you run into memory limits on the \
GPU (unlikely with just text, but possible with massive batches), we can \
easily add a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]chunking\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] loop to \
process them in groups of 50k or 100k.

You are all set for the main computation! Let me know if you need help \
setting up the batch processing or importing the data.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9759143423266582`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"04abe0b2-a9ca-b64b-b44b-\
a906ec3e4e13"],

Cell["\<\
Thank you very much! I will firstly further reduce my ~500,000 word list \
since I already know that some 10k or 20k of them are extendable with very \
long extensions. After this, I will start testing the remaining list in \
relatively small portions to avoid system overheating :) \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9759144593239975`*^9, 3.9759146949624844`*^9}, {
  3.975914736400276*^9, 3.9759147956481113`*^9}, {3.975914847095232*^9, 
  3.975914887495537*^9}, {3.9759149307235165`*^9, 3.975914976665354*^9}, {
  3.9759150651626587`*^9, 3.9759150893179607`*^9}, {3.9759151260402164`*^9, 
  3.97591513027055*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4e05ff96-923f-f144-975e-\
81f64b442d32"],

