Cell[TextData[{
 "Last time the CUDA-code worked beautifully. With it, I extended (in aa2f \
way) all the relevant, suitably reduced, 491903 aa2f (i.e., almost \
abelian-square free) words on three letters. The extensions were quite long, \
8 characters to the left and 8 characters to the right. Even so, the \
computation time was, on average, less than 15 minutes per word lists of \
length 100000. Pretty good, I would say. What is even more important, I could \
check that the all the results regarding non-extendable words were correct! I \
really checked each one of them.  \n\nOut of the 491903 aa2f words about 11.1 \
% could not be extended up to 8 (left) and 8 (right). If the extension would \
be longer, it is very likely that even more of the words turn out not to be \
extendable. With the present method this is not feasible in a reasonable \
computing time, though. That is why, we would need another method. \n\nThis \
another method should proceed as follows. Take the given word ",
 StyleBox["w",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " and extend it with one letter to the left. For testing, it may be easier \
take the mirror image of ",
 StyleBox["w",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " first. Try ",
 StyleBox["a",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["b",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["c",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " in this alphabetical order and take the first letter, say ",
 StyleBox["x",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", that makes the extension aa2f. Next extend this",
 StyleBox[" xw",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " in aa2f way to the right \.08with one letter, say ",
 StyleBox["y",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". Then extend this ",
 StyleBox["xwy",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to the left, then to the right, and so on, up to given extension boundary. \
If no extension work at some point, backtrack, alternating sides all the \
time, as long as is needed and \.08change the last possible letter \
occurrence. If nothing can be changed, classify the given word ",
 StyleBox["w",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " as a ",
 StyleBox["non-extendable word",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", or simply as ",
 StyleBox["bad",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ".  If the given extension boundary ",
 StyleBox["eb",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " (same ",
 StyleBox["eb",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " for left and right) is achieved, the given word ",
 StyleBox["w",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " is classified as ",
 StyleBox["maybe ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "(so-far-so-good). At this point we do not know whether ",
 StyleBox["good",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " words exist in this three letter case. Later, it may be necessary to add a \
third category ",
 StyleBox["undecided",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ",",
 " because the aim is to extend the words with quite long prefixes and \
suffixes (i.e., with quite large extension boundaries ",
 StyleBox["eb",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ").\n\nCould you please design such a function for CUDA computing?"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9775825787979736`*^9, 3.9775825970779743`*^9}, {
  3.977583196243641*^9, 3.977583226837988*^9}, {3.977583529749319*^9, 
  3.9775836166975384`*^9}, {3.977583647214239*^9, 3.977583716327202*^9}, {
  3.9775838078529625`*^9, 3.9775840614929924`*^9}, {3.977584322957655*^9, 
  3.977584466737335*^9}, {3.977584501487301*^9, 3.9775847591709805`*^9}, {
  3.977584798379593*^9, 3.9775853458355446`*^9}, {3.977585392341007*^9, 
  3.977585614548279*^9}, {3.977585668095003*^9, 3.97758571012261*^9}, {
  3.9775857448835106`*^9, 3.97758591640901*^9}, {3.977585948034813*^9, 
  3.9775859622548695`*^9}, {3.9775859951585026`*^9, 3.9775862764530582`*^9}, {
  3.9775863084741116`*^9, 3.97758631654817*^9}, {3.977586348250223*^9, 
  3.9775863810082417`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a2cf5dac-c098-8443-9c93-\
93cf007eff86"],

Cell["\<\
Last time the CUDA - code worked beautifully . With it, I extended (in aa2f \
way) all the relevant, suitably reduced, 491903 aa2f (i . e ., almost abelian \
- square free) words on three letters . The extensions were quite long, 8 \
characters to the left and 8 characters to the right . Even so, the \
computation time was, on average, less than 15 minutes per word list of \
length 100000. Pretty good, I would say . What is even more important, I \
could check that all the results regarding non - extendable words were \
correct! I really checked each one of them . Out of the 491903 aa2f words, \
about 11.1 % could not be extended up to 8 (left) and 8 (right) . If the \
extension were longer, it is very likely that even more of the words would \
turn out not to be extendable . With the present method, this is not feasible \
within a reasonable amount of time, though . That is why we would need \
another method . This other method should proceed as follows . Given the word \
w, extend it by one letter to the left . For testing, it may be easier take \
the mirror image of w first . Try a, b and c in this alphabetical order, and \
take the first letter, say x, that makes the extension aa2f . Next, extend \
this xw in aa2f way to the right with one letter, say y . Then extend this \
xwy to the left, then to the right, and so on, up to the given extension \
boundary . If there is no correct extension at some point, backtrack, \
alternating sides as needed, and change the last possible letter occurrence . \
If nothing can be changed, classify the given word w as a non - extendable \
word, or simply as bad . If the given extension boundary eb (same eb for left \
and right) is achieved, the given word w is classified as maybe (so - far - \
so - good) . At this point, we do not know whether good words exist in this \
three - letter case . Later, it may be necessary to add a third category, \
undecided, because the aim is to extend words with quite long prefixes and \
suffixes (i . e ., with quite large extension boundaries, eb), and \
computation time could become too long . Could you please implement such a \
function for CUDA?\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9775869324358025`*^9, 3.9775869396837616`*^9}, {
  3.9776269668866024`*^9, 
  3.9776269865837975`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"aff6eb84-0bb3-6b4d-8bc1-\
aa63df477ad1"],

Cell["The working code is at the end of this notebook.", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977636819363888*^9, 
  3.9776368369381847`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"18f5d8e4-c8dc-ee46-8086-\
bb40a54d2ab5"],

