Cell[TextData[{
 "The Blackwell Advantage\nAh, an NVIDIA RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell! That \
completely explains the sheer throughput. With over 10,000 CUDA cores, ECC \
memory, and that massive GDDR7 bandwidth, you are essentially driving a \
supercomputer. You were absolutely right to override my conservative 500-word \
suggestion and throw 10,000 words at it. Your hardware \
didn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t even blink, crunching through the batch in just 43.1 \
seconds.\n\nBreaking Down the Probe Data\nLooking closely at your Phase 1 \
through 6 results, there are a few highly revealing mathematical and \
computational trends here for the depth-60 search space:\n\nThe 13% Wall: \
Exactly 1,300 words (13%) timed out at the ",
 StyleBox["50,000",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "-step limit. If this ratio holds across your entire master list of 232,534 \
words, you can expect roughly ~30,000 words to require deep, heavy \
computation.\n\nFast Failures: Notice that all 350 of the \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]NonExtendable\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] dead ends were \
found in Phase 1, and zero were found in Phases 2 through 6. This tells us \
that if a branch is going to die at depth 60, it dies fast. The strings that \
survive Phase 1 and get passed to Phase 2 are incredibly dense and rich with \
valid paths.\n\nThe Time Estimate: 0.3 hours (about 18 minutes) for the full \
232k list is amazing, but remember this is only the estimate if you kept the \
limit at ",
 StyleBox["50,000",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " steps. Since we need to raise that limit to resolve the remaining 13%, the \
actual production run will take longer.\n\nNext Step: The High-Step Pressure \
Test\nYou are exactly on the right track: we need to test these specific \
1,300 \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]stubborn\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] words with a \
much larger step limit to see where their true ceiling is.\n\nWe can pass \
this directly from your probeReport into a new run. I recommend pushing \
maxSteps up to 2,000,000 for this test. Since we are testing deeper limits, I \
would drop the batch size slightly to 1,300 just to let the GPU process them \
in one clean sweep without hitting any single-kernel timeout thresholds.\n\n\
Here is the code block to immediately pressure-test those exact survivors:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9930426306082*^9},
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.95, 0.43, 0.96, 
   0.33],ExpressionUUID->"eb04149a-60ab-304a-9409-abede6f7ce0e"],

