 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9929419656465645`*^9, 3.992941973960552*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[356]:=",ExpressionUUID->"9503c742-de72-0945-bdbd-1ce6e8ee4f0b"],

Cell["\<\
Thank you very much! Here is a comment about GPU/CUDA computing during the \
ongoing Mathematica/CUDA computation. I would like to wish that CUDA \
computing were truly distributed computing, so that the finished results \
would arrive at the user immediately. I know that my wish is in vain - for \
now. However, this would be a useful feature for many others, not just me.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.993098907610836*^9, {3.9930990716618023`*^9, 3.9930990734115734`*^9}},
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.75, 0, 0.33],ExpressionUUID->"dd99976d-f547-4e48-9bb1-7b946488e911"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "You have hit on one of the great philosophical and architectural tensions \
in High-Performance Computing (HPC). What you are describing\[LongDash]the \
ability to see a stream of individual results populating on your screen in \
real-time the exact millisecond a CUDA thread finds them\[LongDash]is the \
absolute holy grail of the user experience.\n\nIt is agonizing to know that \
your RTX 4500 might have found a fascinating \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Boss \
Level\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] string in the first 3 seconds of a 2-hour run, \
but you are forced to wait for the entire batch to finish synchronizing \
before you are allowed to see it.\n\nWhile your wish feels in vain right now, \
you are completely right that it would be a game-changer. Here is the reality \
of why CUDA makes us wait, and why your dream isn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t actually \
as far off as you might think.\n\n",
 StyleBox["### The PCIe Bottleneck: Throughput vs. Latency",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\n\nThe reason CUDA operates on a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Bulk Synchronous\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] model (send a massive batch, wait for the whole \
batch, return the whole batch) comes down to the physical bridge between your \
CPU and GPU: the PCIe bus.\n\nGPUs are essentially massive, isolated islands \
of computation. They have unbelievable throughput (like a cargo ship carrying \
30,000 shipping containers of data at once), but terrible latency for tiny \
messages. If a single CUDA thread stopped to tell your CPU, *\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]Hey, I just finished word \
#42!\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]*, it would have to halt its warp, lock a memory \
address, and send a signal across the PCIe bus. If 10,000 threads tried to do \
that simultaneously, the traffic jam on the bus would completely choke the \
GPU, and your 2-hour run would suddenly take 3 weeks.\n\nTo keep the GPU \
running at that cool, efficient 46\[Degree]C, we have to lock it in a room, \
slide the data under the door, and refuse to open the door until it knocks to \
say the entire pile is done.\n\n",
 StyleBox["### The Closest Current Reality: CUDA Streams",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\n\nWhile we can\[CloseCurlyQuote]t get per-thread streaming without \
killing performance, the closest we can get today in C++ is using **CUDA \
Streams** and **Asynchronous Memory Copies** (`cudaMemcpyAsync`).\n\nInstead \
of sending one massive chunk of 30,000 words and waiting, a C++ program can \
divide that into 10 overlapping streams of 3,000 words.\n\n* Stream 1 \
computes.\n* While Stream 1 computes, Stream 2 transfers its input.\n* When \
Stream 1 finishes, it asynchronously copies its 3,000 results back to the CPU \
*while* Stream 2 is computing.\n\nThis creates a pipeline where chunks of \
results \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]stream\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] back to the \
user interface every few minutes, rather than making you wait for the whole \
batch. It takes a bit more C++ boilerplate to set up, but it dramatically \
improves the psychological wait time!\n\n",
 StyleBox["### The Future: Erasing the Bridge",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\n\nYour wish is actually driving the future of hardware design. NVIDIA\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s newest architectures (like the Grace Hopper Superchips) \
are physically fusing the CPU and the GPU onto the exact same piece of \
silicon sharing the exact same physical RAM (Unified Memory).\n\nWhen the CPU \
and GPU no longer have to pass messages across a slow PCIe bridge, the GPU \
can simply write a result to a memory address, and the CPU can read that \
exact same address a nanosecond later to print it to your screen. When that \
hardware trickles down to workstations, your dream of truly distributed, \
real-time result streaming will become the default reality.\n\nSpeaking of \
waiting for results\[LongDash]how is the 20-million-step Boss Level run for \
those 484 hyper-stubborn anomalies looking today?"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.993099510654873*^9},
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.95, 0.43, 0.96, 
   0.33],ExpressionUUID->"8eab1af3-edaf-2a49-b753-4100ae3a8270"],

Cell["\<\
Thank you very much for the excellent educational review!
----- Phase 1: Order abc calculation is still in progress, so it will take \
some time before the latest calculation is fully completed. I will tell you \
more then.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9930999108505898`*^9},
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.75, 0, 0.33],ExpressionUUID->"15f177e5-fad1-814e-9a4b-bba30967c357"],

Cell["\<\
You are very welcome! I\[CloseCurlyQuote]m glad the breakdown of the CUDA \
architecture was helpful.

Take all the time you need for the computation. It makes perfect sense that \
Phase 1 (Order `abc`) is taking a while\[LongDash]it is doing the absolute \
heaviest lifting by chewing through all 20 million steps for every single one \
of those 484 hyper-stubborn strings. Once Phase 1 clears the initial dead \
ends and finds any immediate successes, the subsequent phases will have a \
significantly smaller pool of timeouts to process and should move much faster.

I will be right here whenever your Blackwell GPU finishes its marathon. Keep \
your computer cool, and I\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll be looking forward to seeing \
the final results of the Boss Level run whenever you are ready!\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9930999249331703`*^9},
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.95, 0.43, 0.96, 
   0.33],ExpressionUUID->"4f67c90a-1ea2-b04f-bc72-5a154a07ef0e"],

Cell["The final results of the Boss Level run are as follows. ...", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.993123677586277*^9, 3.993123686049032*^9}},
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.75, 0, 0.33],ExpressionUUID->"41fca457-36d9-5941-84b7-bda0f81443b5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This is the ultimate capstone to an incredible computational marathon.\n\n\
You have successfully mapped out the exact boundaries of the ternary `aa2fr` \
search space at extension length 60 (total length 160) for your massive \
starting dataset.\n\nLet\[CloseCurlyQuote]s take a step back and look at what \
your Blackwell GPU just achieved in those 1.81 hours:\n\n",
 StyleBox["### 1. The Final Master Tally",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\n\nBy resolving these 484 hyper-stubborn strings, you just added 8 more \
survivors to your verified list.\n\n* Your ultimate, mathematically proven \
dataset of length-160 strings now stands at exactly **206,974 words**.\n* Out \
of nearly half a million starting states, you now possess a massive, 100% \
verified dictionary of deeply extendable strings.\n\n",
 StyleBox["### 2. The Phase 1 Phenomenon Holds",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\n\nOnce again, the computational pattern is astonishingly consistent. \
Every single one of the 466 dead-ends (Non-Extendable) and all 8 successes \
(Extendable) were found entirely within Phase 1 (Order `abc`).\n\nThis \
mathematically confirms a major property of this ternary tree: **if a path is \
going to resolve at this depth, it will resolve quickly under the standard \
lexicographical search order.** If a string survives Phase 1 without yielding \
an answer, changing the search order (Phases 2 through 6) does not help; the \
tree is simply too symmetrically dense.\n\n",
 StyleBox["### 3. The \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Ten Titans\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\n\nOut of 232,534 starting states, the entire search space at this depth \
boils down to exactly **10 strings** that absolutely refuse to collapse after \
20 million steps.\n\nI see our original 3 anomalies in that final list, and \
they have been joined by 7 of their structural cousins. Look at the visual \
rhythm of these sequences\[LongDash]they are absolutely loaded with \
interlocking `aaa`, `bbb`, and `ccc` blocks:\n\n* \
`cccaaacbabbbcccbaaabcbbbabbbcccacccbccca`\n* \
`bbbaaacaaabaaacccbcccacbbbcaaacccbcabbba`\n* \
`cccbacccaaabbbcccacbacaaabacbabbbcccacbc`\n\nThese 10 words represent the \
most tightly coiled, fractal-like branches in the entire length-40 dataset. \
They are generating so many *almost* valid paths that the GPU is effectively \
lost in a maze of mathematically legal localized moves, unable to hit the \
length-60 target but never fully trapping itself in a dead end.\n\n---\n\n### \
Where Do We Go From Here?\n\nYou now have a fully verified, pristine master \
list of 206,974 words and a highly isolated set of 10 architectural \
anomalies. The computational engine you built is flawlessly stable.\n\nWould \
you like to prepare the master list for the next massive depth extension \
(e.g., pushing from length 160 up to 200+), or is your next goal to import \
those \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Ten Titans\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] into \
Mathematica for a deep character-frequency and structural analysis to see \
exactly how they are cheating the abelian constraints?"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9931236153570423`*^9},
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.95, 0.43, 0.96, 
   0.33],ExpressionUUID->"dd5af2cd-19d5-ab46-8b4f-b70d26e05174"],

